src
===

The source code of Pyxplot. The files in this directory hold the program's
entry point and the machinery which ties its parts together: reading input,
reading data files, the multiplot canvas, and LaTeX typesetting. Each
subdirectory, described below, has its own README.

canvasItems.c, canvasItems.h
    The multiplot canvas: the commands which add items to it (plot, text,
    arrow, image and so on) and manage it (list, delete, move, swap, clear).
    Each item records everything needed to redraw it, and the canvas is redrawn
    by epsMaker after each change.

children.c, children.h
    Pyxplot's child processes: the child support process, which owns the
    temporary directory and keeps postscript viewers such as gv running, and
    the short-lived helpers (latex, sed, input filters) connected by pipes.

datafile.c, datafile.h
    Reads data for plot, fit, tabulate and similar commands, from files, from
    vectors or by sampling functions, applying modifiers such as using,
    select, every and index.

datafile_rasters.c, datafile_rasters.h
    Chooses where to sample the step functions made by the histogram and fft
    commands, so that they are sampled once per bin or grid point rather than
    uniformly.

input.c, input.h
    Reads commands, interactively with readline or from scripts, joins
    continued lines, and passes each line to the parser for execution.

pyxplot.c, pyxplot.h
    The entry point of Pyxplot: reads the command-line options, sets up the
    session's context, child processes and settings, then runs each script
    given, or an interactive session.

readConf.c, readConf.h
    Reads the configuration files ~/.pyxplotrc and ./.pyxplotrc, which may
    change the default settings, colors, units and LaTeX preamble, and define
    variables and functions at startup.

texify.c, texify.h
    Converts expressions and strings into LaTeX, for the texify() function and
    for automatically generated text such as the titles of datasets.

pplConstants.h
    Constants used throughout Pyxplot, such as buffer sizes and limits on the
    numbers of items of various kinds.

help.xml
    The text of Pyxplot's interactive help system, read by the help command.

pyxplot.eps
    Pyxplot's logo, as encapsulated postscript.

commands/
    The implementation of each of Pyxplot's commands, from set and show to fit,
    histogram and fft.

coreUtils/
    General-purpose infrastructure: data structures, memory management, error
    reporting and access to the operating system.

defaultObjs/
    The functions, constants, units and modules which are defined when Pyxplot
    starts, and the methods of each type of object.

epsMaker/
    Turns the multiplot canvas into postscript, drawing graphs and other items
    and typesetting their text with LaTeX, then converts it to other formats.

expressions/
    Compiles algebraic expressions into bytecode, and executes it.

helpers/
    Small stand-alone programs built and installed alongside Pyxplot.

mathsTools/
    Small mathematical helpers which the standard C maths library lacks.

parser/
    The command-line parser, which matches each line of input against the
    grammar of Pyxplot's commands.

settings/
    The values changed by the set command, their defaults, and the tables of
    keywords they can take.

stringTools/
    Low-level string handling, used by every other part of Pyxplot.

userspace/
    Pyxplot's data model: the pplObj structure which holds every value, its
    types, physical units, dates, and the state of a session.
